On Sunday, July 5, 2020 at 4:06:11 PM UTC+8, Yuhui Zhang wrote:
> Thanks, Phil, so with a simple structure for a bidimensional model with items load on only one dimension, when computing MDISC one would not need to use the formula √ sum(a1i2 + a2i2), with i denotes item i?  Thank you for your assistance!
>
> On Tuesday, June 30, 2020 at 8:39:52 PM UTC+8, Phil Chalmers wrote:
> > I don't follow why they would be any different. If you have  a simple structure like [a1 0], then MDISC = √ a12 + 02 = a1, so the unidimensional and multidimensional translations of the difficulty parameter will be the same since the denominator is the same (a1 == MDISC).
> >
> > Phil
> >
> >
> > On Tue, Jun 30, 2020 at 7:18 AM Yuhui Zhang <yukfai...@gmail.com> wrote:
> > > Thanks, Phil.  The model is a bi-dimensional one.  Is it incorrect to use the multidimensional formula to compute MDIFF in this case when the items load on only one dimension of this bi-dimensional model?  I used the former and got different results from those with the unidimensional formula, the two sets of results have a correlation of .5.  Much thanks.
> > >
> > > On Tuesday, June 30, 2020 at 12:01:12 AM UTC+8, Phil Chalmers wrote:
> > > > You can just use the unidimensional formula to compute the MDIFF criteria, though mathematically both formula will give the same answer. HTH.
> > > >
> > > > Phil
> > > >
> > > >
> > > > On Thu, Jun 18, 2020 at 2:49 PM Yuhui Zhang <yukfai...@gmail.com> wrote:
> > > > > I'd like to add that I used the second method using the multidimensional discrimination index to compute mdiff and the two sets of mdiff have a correlation of about .5.
> > > > >
> > > > > On Friday, June 19, 2020 at 2:33:23 AM UTC+8, Yuhui Zhang wrote:
> > > > > >
> > > > > >
> > > > > > Hi there,
> > > > > >
> > > > > > I'd like to ask a question about the computation of MDIFF in a bidimensional IRT in which items load only on ONE dimension.   The MDIFF output in the MIRT package seems to give output with b = -d/a.  I read in Reckase that to compute b, one would use b = -d/multidimensional discrimination index.  The multidimensional discrimination index is computed by the squared root of the sum of the squared loadings for all the factors.  Would one use b = -d/a for models with items load exclusively on one dimension, or should one use b =-d/multidimensional discrimination index?  Thanks.
